FreeBSD-SA-26:78.gssd                                       Security Advisory
                                                          The FreeBSD Project

Topic:          gssd maps unrecognized Kerberos principals to UID 0

Category:       core
Module:         gssd
Announced:      2026-11-17
Credits:        Erik Power
Credits:        SecBuddyF, Tencent KeenLab
Affects:        FreeBSD 15.1
Corrected:      2026-11-17 17:05:52 UTC (stable/15, 15.2-STABLE)
                2026-11-17 17:08:08 UTC (releng/15.1, 15.1-RELEASE-p7)
CVE Name:       CVE-2026-101315

For general information regarding FreeBSD Security Advisories,
including descriptions of the fields above, security branches, and the
following sections, please visit <URL:https://security.FreeBSD.org/>.

I.   Background

gssd(8) performs Generic Security Service Application Program Interface
(GSS-API) operations on behalf of the kernel, allowing NFS to use Kerberos
authentication.  When a client establishes a Kerberos-authenticated
session, gssd(8) on the NFS server maps the client's principal to a local
user, whose credentials are used for the session.  Principals with no
corresponding local user are mapped to the unprivileged user "nobody".

II.  Problem Description

When gssd(8) could not map a principal to a local user, it failed to set
the user ID of the resulting credentials, leaving it as zero.  The NFS
server does not apply the exports(5) -maproot and -mapall options to
Kerberos-authenticated requests, so the principal was granted superuser
access.

III. Impact

A remote user holding valid Kerberos credentials for a principal that does
not map to a local user on the NFS server can read and modify any file on
file systems exported with Kerberos security.

IV.  Workaround

No workaround is available.  Only NFS servers running gssd(8) and exporting
file systems with Kerberos security (the krb5, krb5i or krb5p flavors of
the exports(5) -sec option) are affected.

V.   Solution

Upgrade your vulnerable system to a supported FreeBSD stable or
release / security branch (releng) dated after the correction date and
restart gssd(8).

Perform one of the following:

1) To update your vulnerable system installed from base system packages:

Systems running a 15.0-RELEASE or later version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or
arm64 platforms, which were installed using base system packages, can be
updated via the pkg(8) utility:

# pkg upgrade -r FreeBSD-base
# service gssd restart

2) To update your vulnerable system installed from binary distribution sets:

Systems running a RELEASE version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or arm64 platforms
which were not installed using base system packages can be updated via the
freebsd-update(8) utility:

# freebsd-update fetch
# freebsd-update install
# service gssd restart

3) To update your vulnerable system via a source code patch:

The following patches have been verified to apply to the applicable
FreeBSD release branches.

a) Download the relevant patch from the location below, and verify the
detached PGP signature using your PGP utility.

# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/SA-26:78/gssd.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/SA-26:78/gssd.patch.asc
# gpg --verify gssd.patch.asc

b) Apply the patch.  Execute the following commands as root:

# cd /usr/src
# patch -E -p0 < /path/to/patch

c) Recompile the operating system using buildworld and installworld as
described in <URL:https://www.FreeBSD.org/handbook/makeworld.html>.

Restart the applicable daemons, or reboot the system.

VI.  Correction details

This issue is corrected as of the corresponding Git commit hash in the
following stable and release branches:

Branch/path                             Hash                     Revision
- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
stable/15/                              9bbbb1a74dc2    stable/15-n286863
releng/15.1/                            0ed72563c53b  releng/15.1-n283666
- -------------------------------------------------------------------------

Run the following command to see which files were modified by a
particular commit:

# git show --stat <commit hash>

Or visit the following URL, replacing NNNNNN with the hash:

<URL:https://cgit.freebsd.org/src/commit/?id=NNNNNN>

To determine the commit count in a working tree (for comparison against
nNNNNNN in the table above), run:

# git rev-list --count --first-parent HEAD

